{"connection_id": "BC-4e96b00874d8f698a6e4", "ref": "Mishnah Avot 4:22", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "give an account to the King", "note": "Avot says a person will eventually give a reckoning before the King of kings; this parallels the commentary's inevitable personal settlement after one's allotted life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8c004d6ac9b0f0559510", "ref": "Mishnah Rosh Hashanah 1:2", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "all pass before divine judgment", "note": "The Mishnah says all inhabitants of the world pass before God for judgment at the new year; this interprets the commentary's every-person account through a recurring Jewish liturgical day."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a574dc21a07d785db7cb", "ref": "Rosh Hashanah 16b", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "three books; annual judgment", "note": "The commentary distinguishes the people who escape loss from those condemned; the rabbinic New Year judgment tradition pictures the righteous, wicked, and intermediate as entered in three books, giving a developed Jewish account of classification at judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e62ac18e71f9fd0d2d18", "ref": "Shabbat 31a", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "questions at heavenly judgment", "note": "The commentary makes faith and work answerable at the end; the Talmud imagines the court asking whether one dealt faithfully, fixed time for Torah, hoped for salvation, and pursued wisdom, turning judgment into concrete questions about a lived life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-36c8ef535aaabad4ea13", "ref": "Shavuot 39a", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "mutual responsibility; Israel as guarantors", "note": "The commentary makes people urge one another toward truth and patience; Shavuot says all Israel are guarantors for one another, adding a covenantal account of why a person's conduct concerns the whole community."}
{"connection_id": "BC-20f847b3f3c67bca0998", "ref": "Sirach 11:26", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "recompense at death according to conduct", "note": "Sirach says it is easy for the Lord to repay a person at death according to that person's ways; this joins the commentary's allotted term and binding individual recompense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-76d8527d7337d07d0bc8", "ref": "Testament of Abraham, Recension A 13", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "souls; good and evil deeds on scales", "note": "The commentary's balance tests work and separates outcomes; Abraham's vision of judgment has a soul's good and evil deeds weighed, with the result determining its fate, giving a close Jewish apocalyptic scene."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e5fca2cf1473f67a3c5b", "ref": "Testament of Abraham, Recension A 13", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "yorum_gelenegi", "basis": "soul weighed in heavenly judgment", "note": "A scene in this recension appears to bring a soul's merits and sins into a heavenly weighing, close to the commentary's personal balance; the chapter placement and the roles of the heavenly figures are uncertain from memory."}
{"connection_id": "BC-25cc7e64ba2af98d1389", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.2.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God weighs actions", "note": "Hannah's song says the Lord is a God of knowledge and actions are weighed by him; this gives a direct biblical image for the commentary's scales measuring each person's work."}
{"connection_id": "BC-93c2a007249233aaf81c", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.2.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "qere לו; deeds measured by God", "note": "In Hannah's song the traditional qere makes God the one by whom deeds are measured, closely matching the commentary's scales for human action; WLC's written ketiv ולא differs, so this connection depends on the named reading."}
{"connection_id": "BC-5944ed4fa75672a71e2c", "ref": "WLC:1Sam.2.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "WLC ketiv ולא versus qere לו", "note": "The written ketiv can be read as denying that deeds are measured, unlike the commentary's scales; the familiar qere gives the opposite sense, so the reversal is textually uncertain and should be checked against the WLC stream."}
