item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-bb33c9a339a24ad270b9", "ref": "WLC:Dan.5.28", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "weighed king's realm divided", "note": "Daniel's third explanation says Belshazzar's kingdom is divided and given away; it specifies what is lost when the weighed account in the preceding verse proves deficient."}
{"connection_id": "BC-316193d804109759ee2e", "ref": "WLC:Dan.6.29", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Aramaic צלח; table sound correspondence", "note": "Daniel prospers after his deliverance from the lions; this gives a narrative setting for good outcome after steadfastness beside the commentary's good work and patience, without making the roots proven cognates."}
{"connection_id": "BC-57d160b4dce326cfa909", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.10", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "court sits; books opened", "note": "The commentary says deeds are present at judgment; Daniel's heavenly court takes its seat and opens books, adding a courtroom and record-book scene distinct from Daniel's weighed king and resurrection."}
{"connection_id": "BC-adb26095baae2c2c50ac", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.10", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "court sits and books open", "note": "At the heavenly court books are opened before judgment; this corresponds to the commentary's deeds present as a record when each person's account closes."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e5464358b03a0a4df1c8", "ref": "WLC:Dan.7.22", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "judgment for the holy ones", "note": "The commentary separates those whose work preserves them from the lost; after the beasts' oppression Daniel says judgment is given in favor of the holy ones and their time comes to possess the kingdom, adding vindication for the righteous."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8b2107cef2ec4baca8e0", "ref": "WLC:Deut.32.35", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "appointed time of recompense", "note": "Deuteronomy reserves vengeance and repayment for the time when the wrongdoers' foot slips; the commentary likewise lets a fixed time mature into binding recompense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-481f20c2d600eca3a8a7", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.1.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Source root ع م ل; Hebrew עמל; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary labels the deeds with ع م ل and presents work within a profit-and-loss account. Ecclesiastes 1:3 asks what gain a person has from all the toil under the sun; עמל is the package table's unverified regular correspondence, and its sense is toil rather than morally good deed."}
{"connection_id": "BC-73fe0e547bce8d08cc1f", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.1.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew עמל; table sound correspondence", "note": "Qoheleth asks what gain a person gets from all his toil under the sun; the commentary hears human loss beside work and passing time, though this verse questions earthly gain rather than announcing final punishment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-34f4e6c2158b56da89d1", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.11.9", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "joy now; judgment for all", "note": "The commentary treats time as a term ending in reckoning; Ecclesiastes permits the young to follow heart and eyes but warns that God will bring them into judgment for all this, joining present action to future accounting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-328f5022efff09fc4d70", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.12.14", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "all deeds; good and evil", "note": "The commentary says every deed is present for judgment and good is distinguished from evil; Ecclesiastes says God brings every deed, including hidden things, to judgment, whether good or evil."}
{"connection_id": "BC-cb892ee810c7933bf7a1", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.12.14", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "every deed and hidden thing judged", "note": "Qoheleth says God will bring every deed, including hidden good or evil, to judgment; the commentary similarly makes even tiny acts present at the final account."}
{"connection_id": "BC-af09a9b1dc3de3adc51d", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.2.11", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew עמל; table sound correspondence", "note": "Qoheleth surveys his works and toil and finds no lasting gain; this sharpens the commentary's accounting sense of human loss, while its exception for good deeds gives another outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a24dff7c19e2d9c2d3db", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.3.17", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "time to judge every work", "note": "The commentary connects time with the due day and says good and bad works are separated; Ecclesiastes explicitly says there is a time for every matter and work when God judges righteous and wicked."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7ad7421700efafcf308b", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.3.17", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "time for every work and judgment", "note": "Qoheleth says God will judge righteous and wicked because there is a time for every matter and every work; this directly joins the commentary's allotted time, work, and judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-74d66a224e1b17e8c78a", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.9.10", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "work only within mortal life", "note": "The commentary makes the present term the span in which work counts; Ecclesiastes urges doing what one's hand finds with strength because there is no work or knowledge in Sheol, making life's limit a reason for action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3c4537e92799451f36d8", "ref": "WLC:Eccl.9.10", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "work while time remains", "note": "Qoheleth urges doing whatever work one's hand finds because the grave has no work; the commentary likewise treats time as a deadline for deeds that later face accounting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0c8dace87f76c58a7b69", "ref": "WLC:Exod.32.33", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "sinner blotted from divine book", "note": "God says the one who sins against him will be erased from his book; this reverses the commentary's image of acts attached to the person while keeping the same individual-account logic."}
