item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-07bfb618db4b644380f6", "ref": "WLC:Ezek.18.30", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "judged each according to ways", "note": "God says he will judge each Israelite according to that person's ways and calls for repentance; this gives the commentary's individual work-based account a setting where conduct can still change before judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f440a3efde1e88b78c9e", "ref": "WLC:Gen.15.16", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "iniquity not yet complete", "note": "The Amorites' iniquity has not yet reached its full measure, delaying judgment; this provides an appointed accumulation of evil beside the commentary's heap of bad deeds."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a4d27bb0fec310fc4238", "ref": "WLC:Gen.18.25", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "judge of all earth; righteous not swept with wicked", "note": "The commentary says humanity is in loss but preserves an exception; Abraham asks whether the Judge of all earth will destroy the righteous with the wicked, making the justice of a collective sentence depend on distinguishing the righteous."}
{"connection_id": "BC-c39d4bc3a222411fda04", "ref": "WLC:Hab.2.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "appointed time; wait", "note": "The commentary joins a fixed term with patience; Habakkuk says the vision awaits its appointed time and, even if delayed, is to be awaited. Its context is a promised vision, not a final judgment, but it makes waiting an active response to a fixed divine term."}
