{"connection_id": "BC-b7019dc3248138ce73fc", "ref": "WLC:Jer.17.10", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "search heart and repay deeds", "note": "The Lord searches the heart and repays each person according to ways and fruit of deeds; the commentary's day likewise brings each person to the consequence of his work."}
{"connection_id": "BC-95381dc7ce35caf95cdf", "ref": "WLC:Jer.32.19", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "eyes on all ways and deeds", "note": "Jeremiah praises God's eyes upon all human ways so as to give each according to conduct and the fruit of deeds; this joins the commentary's complete record to individual repayment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-587d6ff339199e30261c", "ref": "WLC:Job.14.17", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "transgression sealed in a bag", "note": "Job imagines his transgression sealed in a bag before God; this offers another personal record of wrong beside the commentary's deeds kept for later confrontation."}
{"connection_id": "BC-0b502f5d71d2464610f6", "ref": "WLC:Job.14.5", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "fixed human term", "note": "Job says a person's days are determined and God sets a limit that cannot be passed; this concretizes the commentary's reading of time as a term that brings each person to reckoning."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d38ab42bbdff379f7483", "ref": "WLC:Job.14.5", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Source root ح ق ق; Hebrew חֹק; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary labels Hāqqa with ح ق ق and explains the day as the due, appointed end of each person's account. Job says human days and months are fixed and their limit is set by God; חֹק is from the package table's unverified חקק correspondence, so the shared prescribed-limit sense is specific but etymology remains unproven."}
{"connection_id": "BC-db838da09427ebe2348b", "ref": "WLC:Job.14.5", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew חקק in fixed bounds; table sound correspondence", "note": "Job says human days and their limits have been prescribed and cannot be crossed; this makes concrete the commentary's appointed term within its reading of حَقّ and the day of reckoning, without equating Hebrew statute with Arabic truth."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-2b18769fbaa94e70ddae", "ref": "WLC:Job.27.16", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Hebrew צבר; table sound correspondence; piled wealth", "note": "Job says a wicked person may heap up silver; the next verse transfers what he prepared to the righteous, making material accumulation and final loss an indirect counterpart to the commentary's piled evil. The Hebrew heaping sense is distinct from Arabic patience."}
{"connection_id": "BC-511e67ce14b7f55dd2a9", "ref": "WLC:Job.27.17", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wicked's stores enjoyed by just people", "note": "Job says the just will wear what the wicked prepared; this indirectly reverses the commentary's piled bad deeds into an earthly redistribution rather than an end-time scale."}
{"connection_id": "BC-11cde408f33124a541a6", "ref": "WLC:Job.31.6", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "just balance; integrity", "note": "Job asks to be weighed in a just balance so God may know his integrity; the commentary's scales test deeds, while Job turns the same image into a plea for an honest verdict on a life."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3a06b2f162df293afd50", "ref": "WLC:Job.31.6", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "let God weigh in just scales", "note": "Job asks to be weighed in a just balance so God may know his integrity; the commentary likewise pictures scales settling a person's case by what he has done."}
{"connection_id": "BC-197948271d5720ec1295", "ref": "WLC:Job.34.11", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "repayment according to work and way", "note": "The commentary makes each person answer for work at the appointed reckoning; Job says God repays a person according to his work and makes each find according to his way, a direct formulation of individual moral accounting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2f7874292c6bbb1b4369", "ref": "WLC:Job.34.11", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "each repaid according to work", "note": "Elihu says God repays a person for work and causes each to find the consequence of a way; this states the commentary's personal work-to-recompense formula in another setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ca6ffd9f5cac991ea043", "ref": "WLC:Job.5.7", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew עמל; table sound correspondence", "note": "Job says humans are born to trouble; this shares the commentary's universal human condition but locates it in earthly toil rather than necessarily in punishment for misdeeds."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f700d54797df7ce0de2d", "ref": "WLC:Josh.1.8", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew צלח; table sound correspondence", "note": "Joshua is told to observe and do the law so his way will prosper; the link to the commentary is the joined action and favorable result, with only a proposed sound correspondence between roots."}
{"connection_id": "BC-10cfe4142aec04d10242", "ref": "WLC:Lev.19.17", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rebuke one's neighbor", "note": "The commentary makes truth something people mutually urge on one another; Leviticus commands a person to rebuke a neighbor rather than bear sin with him, giving mutual moral correction a concrete communal form."}
{"connection_id": "BC-79cfdfb5dd3de4997ab2", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.16", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "book of remembrance", "note": "The commentary says each person's work is present at judgment; Malachi describes a record written before God for those who fear him and esteem his name, preserving the words and deeds of a faithful group."}
{"connection_id": "BC-041175f8bce5672239bb", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.16", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "speak together and book of remembrance", "note": "Those who fear the Lord speak to one another and a book of remembrance is written before him; this joins the surah's mutual counsel to the commentary's record of deeds."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8c1a3613b4d7bc652682", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.17", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "appointed day and spared people", "note": "The Lord names the day on which he acts and spares those he calls his own; this supplies a concrete appointed-day counterpart to the commentary's exception from loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-75a12930854bcb8a86df", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.18", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "distinguish righteous and wicked", "note": "The commentary's final sorting of good from evil is echoed by Malachi's promised renewed distinction between the righteous and wicked, specifically between serving God and not serving him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9881357452155ddad6dd", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.18", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "discern righteous and wicked", "note": "At God's coming action people will again distinguish the righteous from the wicked and the one who serves God from one who does not; the commentary likewise ends with good and bad separated by their conduct."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b1ef0031223500cdc21c", "ref": "WLC:Neh.13.14", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "remember deeds and do not blot them", "note": "Nehemiah asks God not to wipe out his deeds done for the temple; the commentary's good works present at judgment have a concrete prayer for preserved memory."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b6b3840fcd8906bdc41a", "ref": "WLC:Prov.11.1", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "honest weights; just scales", "note": "The commentary's judgment uses scales of justice; Proverbs condemns a deceptive balance and delights in a just weight, linking accurate weighing with moral justice in ordinary dealings."}
{"connection_id": "BC-deb056a81b18e7055901", "ref": "WLC:Prov.13.25", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew חסר; table sound correspondence", "note": "The righteous eats to satisfaction while the wicked person's belly lacks; this maps the commentary's good/bad division onto a concrete lack, though Proverbs speaks of present provision."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3790b447e3c5e9806a6b", "ref": "WLC:Prov.14.23", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew חסר; table sound correspondence", "note": "Proverbs contrasts gain from labor with need arising from mere talk, using the table's Hebrew lack root; this is a present-life analogue to the commentary's work and loss account, not an end-time verdict."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e5d2fb8dd473eb17adb5", "ref": "WLC:Prov.16.11", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "just balances belong to God", "note": "Proverbs attributes just scales and weights to the Lord; this anchors the commentary's judgment-day balance in a concrete image of divine fairness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e8ea7a848af7ac584fb1", "ref": "WLC:Prov.16.2", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God weighs spirits", "note": "The commentary's scales test deeds; Proverbs adds that God weighs the spirits behind a person's apparently pure ways, bringing motive as well as outward action under scrutiny."}

