{"connection_id": "BC-3a06b2f162df293afd50", "ref": "WLC:Job.31.6", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "let God weigh in just scales", "note": "Job asks to be weighed in a just balance so God may know his integrity; the commentary likewise pictures scales settling a person's case by what he has done."}
{"connection_id": "BC-197948271d5720ec1295", "ref": "WLC:Job.34.11", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "repayment according to work and way", "note": "The commentary makes each person answer for work at the appointed reckoning; Job says God repays a person according to his work and makes each find according to his way, a direct formulation of individual moral accounting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2f7874292c6bbb1b4369", "ref": "WLC:Job.34.11", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "each repaid according to work", "note": "Elihu says God repays a person for work and causes each to find the consequence of a way; this states the commentary's personal work-to-recompense formula in another setting."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ca6ffd9f5cac991ea043", "ref": "WLC:Job.5.7", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew עמל; table sound correspondence", "note": "Job says humans are born to trouble; this shares the commentary's universal human condition but locates it in earthly toil rather than necessarily in punishment for misdeeds."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f700d54797df7ce0de2d", "ref": "WLC:Josh.1.8", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew צלח; table sound correspondence", "note": "Joshua is told to observe and do the law so his way will prosper; the link to the commentary is the joined action and favorable result, with only a proposed sound correspondence between roots."}
{"connection_id": "BC-10cfe4142aec04d10242", "ref": "WLC:Lev.19.17", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "rebuke one's neighbor", "note": "The commentary makes truth something people mutually urge on one another; Leviticus commands a person to rebuke a neighbor rather than bear sin with him, giving mutual moral correction a concrete communal form."}
{"connection_id": "BC-79cfdfb5dd3de4997ab2", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.16", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "book of remembrance", "note": "The commentary says each person's work is present at judgment; Malachi describes a record written before God for those who fear him and esteem his name, preserving the words and deeds of a faithful group."}
{"connection_id": "BC-041175f8bce5672239bb", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.16", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "speak together and book of remembrance", "note": "Those who fear the Lord speak to one another and a book of remembrance is written before him; this joins the surah's mutual counsel to the commentary's record of deeds."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8c1a3613b4d7bc652682", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.17", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "appointed day and spared people", "note": "The Lord names the day on which he acts and spares those he calls his own; this supplies a concrete appointed-day counterpart to the commentary's exception from loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-75a12930854bcb8a86df", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.18", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "distinguish righteous and wicked", "note": "The commentary's final sorting of good from evil is echoed by Malachi's promised renewed distinction between the righteous and wicked, specifically between serving God and not serving him."}
{"connection_id": "BC-9881357452155ddad6dd", "ref": "WLC:Mal.3.18", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "discern righteous and wicked", "note": "At God's coming action people will again distinguish the righteous from the wicked and the one who serves God from one who does not; the commentary likewise ends with good and bad separated by their conduct."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b1ef0031223500cdc21c", "ref": "WLC:Neh.13.14", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "remember deeds and do not blot them", "note": "Nehemiah asks God not to wipe out his deeds done for the temple; the commentary's good works present at judgment have a concrete prayer for preserved memory."}
{"connection_id": "BC-b6b3840fcd8906bdc41a", "ref": "WLC:Prov.11.1", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "honest weights; just scales", "note": "The commentary's judgment uses scales of justice; Proverbs condemns a deceptive balance and delights in a just weight, linking accurate weighing with moral justice in ordinary dealings."}
{"connection_id": "BC-deb056a81b18e7055901", "ref": "WLC:Prov.13.25", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew חסר; table sound correspondence", "note": "The righteous eats to satisfaction while the wicked person's belly lacks; this maps the commentary's good/bad division onto a concrete lack, though Proverbs speaks of present provision."}
{"connection_id": "BC-3790b447e3c5e9806a6b", "ref": "WLC:Prov.14.23", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew חסר; table sound correspondence", "note": "Proverbs contrasts gain from labor with need arising from mere talk, using the table's Hebrew lack root; this is a present-life analogue to the commentary's work and loss account, not an end-time verdict."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e5d2fb8dd473eb17adb5", "ref": "WLC:Prov.16.11", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "just balances belong to God", "note": "Proverbs attributes just scales and weights to the Lord; this anchors the commentary's judgment-day balance in a concrete image of divine fairness."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e8ea7a848af7ac584fb1", "ref": "WLC:Prov.16.2", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "God weighs spirits", "note": "The commentary's scales test deeds; Proverbs adds that God weighs the spirits behind a person's apparently pure ways, bringing motive as well as outward action under scrutiny."}

item.stdout:
{"connection_id": "BC-21141b3affa17431396e", "ref": "WLC:Prov.16.2", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "apparently pure ways weighed by God", "note": "A person sees his ways as pure, but the Lord weighs the spirits; this probes the commentary's case of people confident their work is good before divine measurement."}
{"connection_id": "BC-304dc2b93c7099e83578", "ref": "WLC:Prov.24.12", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "weigher of hearts; recompense", "note": "The commentary's scales miss no deed; Proverbs says the one who weighs hearts knows and repays each person according to work, joining scrutiny with recompense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e097cc677a95c9daca04", "ref": "WLC:Prov.24.12", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "weigher of hearts repays deeds", "note": "Proverbs says the one who weighs hearts knows excuses and repays each person according to work; this combines the commentary's scales, personal deeds, and binding recompense."}
{"connection_id": "BC-8bd5ab7736deb20b477d", "ref": "WLC:Prov.28.27", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "giving to poor avoids lack", "note": "The one who gives to the poor will not lack, while ignoring them brings curses; this gives the commentary's good-deed exception from loss a material example."}
{"connection_id": "BC-bdeaa6bd80e00adceaef", "ref": "WLC:Prov.3.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "truth bound around the neck", "note": "Proverbs tells the hearer to bind loyalty and truth around the neck; the commentary's record bound to each person's neck meets a contrasting image in which truth is what one chooses to carry."}
{"connection_id": "BC-1bd5c563d6b0e706cefc", "ref": "WLC:Prov.6.21", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "command bound around the neck", "note": "Proverbs tells the hearer to tie parental teaching around the neck; beside the commentary's deed record attached there, this shows a life-guiding text voluntarily carried before judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-ef8b4e94330bb210f41d", "ref": "WLC:Prov.6.32", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew חסר; table sound correspondence", "note": "Proverbs calls the adulterer lacking in sense and says he destroys himself; the commentary's loss through sinful deeds gains a specific self-destructive example, though the Hebrew lack first denotes understanding."}
{"connection_id": "BC-df5faf21600d7aea5eaf", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Source root ص ل ح; Hebrew צלח; table sound correspondence", "note": "The commentary labels the exception's righteous deeds with ص ل ح. In Psalm 1:3 the righteous person's activity prospers; צלח is the package table's unverified regular correspondence, adding a prosperity outcome to the commentary's moral sense of righteous action."}
{"connection_id": "BC-260a88298fe30ea990bd", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.3", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew צלח; table sound correspondence", "note": "The person rooted in the Lord's instruction prospers in what he does; this gives the commentary's righteous works an outcome opposed to loss, although Hebrew prosperity is not identical to Arabic صالح deeds."}
{"connection_id": "BC-53938c2502b9fcef60a4", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.4", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "wicked as wind-driven chaff", "note": "After the fruitful righteous person, the psalm depicts wicked people as chaff driven away by wind; this supplies a contrasting failure of substance beside the commentary's separated losers, without asserting a literal scale."}
{"connection_id": "BC-481d70ec5a08115acaed", "ref": "WLC:Ps.1.6", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "righteous way; wicked perish", "note": "The commentary's exception separates the righteous from those in loss; Psalm 1 ends by contrasting the way known by the Lord with the way of the wicked that perishes, framing the division as two paths."}
{"connection_id": "BC-abaec22688645cdc92b4", "ref": "WLC:Ps.15.2", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "do righteousness and speak truth", "note": "The psalm's qualified guest walks blamelessly, does righteousness, and speaks truth in the heart; this combines good action and truth as the surah's exception does, with access to God's dwelling as its outcome."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7264994af3075e0ad5e5", "ref": "WLC:Ps.34.11", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "soydas", "basis": "Hebrew חסר; table sound correspondence", "note": "The psalm says seekers of the Lord lack no good thing; that absence of lack is a pointed counterpart to the commentary's exception from human loss."}
{"connection_id": "BC-84e02a3e6ae927b4e958", "ref": "WLC:Ps.37.7", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "karsi_anlati", "basis": "Hebrew צלח; table sound correspondence", "note": "The psalm urges waiting before the Lord while a person carrying out wicked schemes prospers; its temporary success reverses the commentary's judgment-day valuation of works."}
{"connection_id": "BC-87817137238b0d0d630c", "ref": "WLC:Ps.39.7", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "Hebrew צבר; table sound correspondence; heaped goods", "note": "The psalm says a person heaps things without knowing who will gather them; this provides a time-limited loss for amassed goods, only an indirect parallel to the commentary's heap of bad works. The Hebrew heaping sense is distinct from Arabic patience."}
{"connection_id": "BC-7bccab5684d30ca7fcd8", "ref": "WLC:Ps.50.21", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "evil set before the sinner", "note": "God says he will set the wrongdoer's acts before his eyes when rebuking him; this closely matches the commentary's claim that one's own deeds confront one at judgment."}
{"connection_id": "BC-e75b4540912c1d3f5711", "ref": "WLC:Ps.62.10", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "humanity light on scales", "note": "The commentary's scales distinguish those whose account is deficient; Psalm 62 says people together are lighter than breath when set on scales. The shared image sharpens a difference: the psalm weighs human worth as vanishing, while the commentary weighs deeds and preserves an exception."}
{"connection_id": "BC-f43aaa5550e7dc932289", "ref": "WLC:Ps.62.10", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "light in the balances", "note": "The psalm places human ranks in balances and finds them lighter than breath; the commentary likewise visualizes human loss as insufficient weight, though the psalm weighs human frailty and pretension."}
{"connection_id": "BC-d5ea840e11747d530fe6", "ref": "WLC:Ps.62.13", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "repaid according to each one's work", "note": "The commentary's deficient account rests on work; this verse says God repays each person according to his work, supplying a distinct recompense formula in the psalm whose earlier verse weighs humanity on scales."}
{"connection_id": "BC-420021d518a5c64951f6", "ref": "WLC:Ps.62.13", "target": "sec13", "tradition": "tevrat", "kind": "motif", "basis": "according to each person's work", "note": "The psalm says the Lord repays a person according to work; this is the same work-to-recompense relation used to explain the commentary's appointed day."}

